To create a Google Form item that shows the unique values from Column A as a multi-select checkbox list, use the following Google Apps Script. It reads Column A, removes blanks and duplicates, then adds a Checkbox item to a new Form so respondents can select one or more options.

1) Open your spreadsheet, then go to Extensions > Apps Script and paste this script:

```javascript
function createFormWithUniqueCheckboxesFromColumnA() {
  const ss = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSpreadsheet();
  const sheet = ss.getActiveSheet();

  // Read only the rows that contain data to avoid scanning whole column
  const lastRow = sheet.getLastRow();
  if (lastRow < 1) {
    Logger.log('No data in sheet');
    return;
  }

  // If your sheet has a header in row 1, start at row 2: startRow = 2
  const startRow = 1;
  const values = sheet.getRange(startRow, 1, lastRow - startRow + 1, 1)
                      .getValues()
                      .map(r => String(r[0]).trim())
                      .filter(v => v !== '');

  // Remove duplicates while preserving insertion order
  const uniqueValues = [...new Set(values)];

  if (uniqueValues.length === 0) {
    Logger.log('No non-empty values found in Column A');
    return;
  }

  // Create the form and add a checkbox item (multi-select)
  const form = FormApp.create('Form - Select one or more');
  const checkboxItem = form.addCheckboxItem();
  checkboxItem.setTitle('Select option(s)');
  checkboxItem.setChoiceValues(uniqueValues);

  // Log URLs so you can open the form
  Logger.log('Form edit URL: ' + form.getEditUrl());
  Logger.log('Form published URL: ' + form.getPublishedUrl());
}
```

2) Save and run the function. The script will ask for authorization to access your Sheet and Forms — grant the permissions.

3) After it runs, open the form using the logged edit URL (View > Logs). The form will contain a checkbox question showing each unique (deduplicated) value from Column A; respondents can check multiple boxes.

Notes and optional tweaks:
- If your sheet has a header row, set startRow = 2 so the header is skipped.
- To update an existing Form instead of creating a new one, open it with FormApp.openById(formId) and use getItems()/asCheckboxItem() to modify or replace the choices.
- You can sort uniqueValues (uniqueValues.sort()) before setChoiceValues() if you want alphabetical order.

